    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e9cb5dc6-f765-5e12-a1d5-b2a00676a7a1",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1x+(2)y?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-4,1)$,$(4,-1)$,$(1,4)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-4,1)}=1(-4)+(2)(1)=-2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(4,-1)}=1(4)+(2)(-1)=2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)}=1(1)+(2)(4)=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/review-3-policy-2-b6fc68f59ef8c5ba/assets/31e1dc3158478949aea1b64aceac4254b7ddf96e7298fa3a4af9ecca96ddf844.png)",
